== How Repentance Is Given

To return to the great text: “God exalted Him to His right hand to be a Prince and a Savior, to give repentance to Israel and forgiveness of sins.” Our Lord Jesus Christ has gone up so that grace may come down. His glory is used to spread his grace more widely. The Lord has not taken one step upward except with the purpose of carrying believing sinners upward with him. He is exalted to give repentance; and we will see this if we remember a few great truths.

#emph[The work our Lord Jesus has done has made repentance possible, available, and acceptable]. The law makes no mention of repentance, but says plainly, “The soul who sins is the one who will die.” If the Lord Jesus had not died and risen again and gone to the Father, what would your repenting or mine be worth? We might feel remorse with its horrors, but never repentance with its hopes. Repentance, as a natural feeling, is a common duty that deserves no great praise: indeed, it is so generally mixed with a selfish fear of punishment that the kindest estimate makes little of it. If Jesus had not stepped in and worked out a wealth of merit, our tears of repentance would have been so much water spilled on the ground. Jesus is exalted on high, so that through the power of his intercession repentance may have a place before God. In this respect he gives us repentance, because he puts repentance in a position of acceptance which it could never otherwise have held.

When Jesus was exalted on high, #emph[the Spirit of God was poured out to work in us all the graces we need]. The Holy Spirit creates repentance in us by renewing our nature supernaturally, and taking away the heart of stone out of our flesh. Oh, do not sit straining those eyeballs of yours to force out impossible tears; repentance does not come from unwilling nature, but from free and sovereign grace. Do not go to your room and beat your breast in order to draw out of a heart of stone feelings which are not there. But go to Calvary and see how Jesus died. Look up to the hills from which your help comes. The Holy Spirit has come for the very purpose of overshadowing men’s spirits and breeding repentance within them, just as he once brooded over chaos and brought order out of it. Breathe your prayer to him, “Blessed Spirit, dwell with me. Make me tender and lowly of heart, that I may hate sin and sincerely repent of it.” He will hear your cry and answer you.

Remember, too, that when our Lord Jesus was exalted, he gave us repentance not only by sending the Holy Spirit, but by #emph[consecrating all the works of nature and of providence to the great purposes of our salvation], so that any one of them may call us to repentance, whether it crows like Peter’s rooster, or shakes the prison like the jailer’s earthquake. From the right hand of God our Lord Jesus rules all things here below, and makes them work together for the salvation of his redeemed. He uses both bitter things and sweet, trials and joys, to produce in sinners a better mind toward their God. Be thankful for the providence which has made you poor, or sick, or sad; for by all this Jesus works the life of your spirit and turns you to himself. The Lord’s mercy often rides to the door of our hearts on the black horse of affliction. Jesus uses the whole range of our experience to wean us from earth and woo us to heaven. Christ is exalted to the throne of heaven and earth so that, by all the workings of his providence, he may bring hard hearts under the gracious softening of repentance.

Besides, he is at work at this very hour by all his whispers in the conscience, by his inspired Book, by those of us who speak out of that Book, and by praying friends and earnest hearts. He can send you a word which will strike your rocky heart as with the rod of Moses, and make streams of repentance flow. He can bring to your mind some heartbreaking text of Holy Scripture which will conquer you very quickly. He can mysteriously soften you, and cause a holy frame of mind to steal over you when you least expect it. Be sure of this: he who has gone into his glory, raised into all the splendor and majesty of God, has abundant ways of working repentance in those to whom he grants forgiveness. #emph[He is even now waiting to give repentance to you]. Ask him for it at once.

Notice, to your great comfort, that the #emph[Lord Jesus Christ gives this repentance to the most unlikely people in the world]. He is exalted to give repentance to #emph[Israel]. To Israel! In the days when the apostles spoke this way, Israel was the nation which had most grossly sinned against light and love, and had crowned its guilt by crucifying the Lord, and daring to say, “His blood be on us and on our children!” Why, these were the murderers of Jesus; and yet he is exalted to give #emph[them] repentance! What a marvel of grace! Listen, then. If you have been brought up in the brightest Christian light, and yet have rejected it, there is still hope. If you have sinned against conscience, and against the Holy Spirit, and against the love of Jesus, there is still room for repentance. Though you may be as hard as unbelieving Israel of old, softening may yet come to you, since Jesus is exalted, and clothed with boundless power. For those who went furthest in iniquity, and sinned with especially aggravated guilt, the Lord Jesus is exalted to give them repentance and forgiveness of sins. How happy I am to have so full a gospel to proclaim! How happy you are to be allowed to hear it!

The hearts of the children of Israel had grown as hard as flint. Luther used to think it impossible to convert a Jew. We are far from agreeing with him, and yet we must admit that the people of Israel have been exceedingly obstinate in rejecting the Savior through these many centuries. Truly the Lord said, “Israel would have none of Me.” “He came to His own, and His own did not receive Him.” Yet on behalf of Israel our Lord Jesus is exalted to give repentance and remission. My reader is probably a Gentile; but he may still have a very stubborn heart, which has held out against the Lord Jesus for many years; and yet our Lord can work repentance in him. It may be that you too will one day feel compelled to write as William Hone did when he yielded to divine love. He was the author of those most entertaining volumes called the “Every-day Book,” but he was once a stubborn unbeliever. When he was subdued by sovereign grace, he wrote:

#quote[“The proudest heart that ever beat \ Hath been subdued in me; \ The wildest will that ever rose \ To scorn thy cause and aid thy foes \ Is quell’d, my Lord, by thee.]

#quote[“Thy will, and not my will be done, \ My heart be ever thine; \ Confessing thee the mighty Word, \ My Savior Christ, my God, my Lord, \ Thy cross shall be my sign.”]

The Lord can give repentance to the most unlikely, turning lions into lambs, and ravens into doves. Let us seek him, that this great change may be worked in us. Certainly, thinking about the death of Christ is one of the surest and quickest ways of gaining repentance. Do not sit down and try to pump up repentance from the dry well of corrupt nature. It is against the laws of the mind to suppose that you can force your soul into that gracious state. Take your heart in prayer to him who understands it, and say, “Lord, cleanse it. Lord, renew it. Lord, work repentance in it.” The more you try to produce feelings of penitence in yourself, the more you will be disappointed; but if, believing, you think of Jesus dying for you, repentance will burst out. Meditate on the Lord’s shedding his heart’s blood out of love for you. Set before your mind’s eye the agony and bloody sweat, the cross and suffering; and, as you do this, he who bore all this grief will look at you, and with that look he will do for you what he did for Peter, so that you also will go out and weep bitterly. He who died for you can, by his gracious Spirit, make you die to sin; and he who has gone into glory on your behalf can draw your soul after him, away from evil, and toward holiness.

I will be content if I leave this one thought with you: do not look under the ice to find fire, nor hope to find repentance in your own natural heart. Look to the Living One for life. Look to Jesus for all you need between Hell Gate and Heaven Gate. Never look anywhere else for any part of what Jesus loves to give; but remember,

#align(center)[#smallcaps[Christ is all].]
